# The weight p of the choice is a program variable, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p >= 0.  Where p >= 2 the choice is reached with weight p outside [0, 1]: such a
# run is an error from which no bound may be proved, so against an upper bound it is worth +oo
# and the bound fails at every p >= 2.  Elsewhere c = 0 and the bound holds.
# Expected: unknown.  When an out-of-range weight made the run abort (worth 0 in both
# directions) the preexpectation was 0 at p >= 2 and the bound was proved (valid); clamping the
# weight to 1 would give E[c] = 1 there, also above 0.5.
nat p;
nat c;
c := 0;
if (p >= 2) {
	{
		c := 1;
	}[p]{
		c := 0;
	}
} else {
	c := 0;
}
?Ex [c] <= 0.5
